<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Ekron</h1><p class="st-meta">1175 BCE – 604 BCE (approx.) · capital Ekron</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ekron, a Philistine city that grew into the largest olive-oil producer of the ancient Near East under Assyrian rule.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ekron, at Tel Miqne, was refounded in the 12th century BCE by newcomers whose pottery, hearths and diet show roots in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> world. They built a planned town that formed one of the five cities of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/philistine-pentapolis">Philistine Pentapolis</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ekron shrank in the 10th century BCE, then boomed in the 7th as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>, with over a hundred olive presses producing oil for export. A dedication inscription found in 1996 names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikausu">Ikausu</a> son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padi">Padi</a> as king and the goddess Ptgyh, confirming the city's identity.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 701 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padi">Padi</a> was handed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah</a> by rebels, then restored by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a> destroyed Ekron in 604 BCE, ending the city.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ekron.jpg" alt="Ekron" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: אבישי טייכר · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PikiWiki_Israel_5617_phillistine_street_in_revadim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1175 BCE – 604 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ekron</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Padi, Ikausu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Philistine religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Philistine, Canaanite</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Philistine Ekron, Tel Miqne</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-890&amp;focus=ekron" aria-label="Ekron on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5578 1021 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ekron lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5898 1277L6092 1134M5898 1277L6140 1251"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6092 1134h0M6140 1251h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5898 1277h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5898 1277h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-890&amp;focus=ekron">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

